fix(deploy): keep the trust root out of reach of the teardown

A teardown with volumes removed took the ledger from 19,595 chain positions
to 60 and the transparency log from 525 entries to 3, and minted a new Fulcio
CA key and a new Rekor signing key. Commits signed the day before stopped
verifying. The signature is intact and the trailer still matches the
certificate; the CA that issued it and the log that recorded it are gone.
There is no repair. It happened twice the same day.

A compose project's volumes are exactly what `down -v` removes, and the CA
key, the log key, the log and the ledger were all in them. So:

scripts/teardown-guard.sh refuses any command that would remove a volume
holding a key or the chain, names what it would have destroyed, and takes
INNSEGL_DESTROY_TRUST_ROOT=<volume>[,...] from an operator who means it.
Naming the volumes is the opt-out, and a volume at risk that is not named
still refuses. Every teardown target in the Makefile goes through it.

deploy/compose/trust-volumes.sh creates the irreplaceable volumes outside the
compose project, stamps them with one deployment id, and migrates an existing
deployment's bytes into them with the stack down. A set that does not agree
on its id is refused rather than adopted.

deploy/compose/sigstore.yml and innsegl.yml declare those volumes external
when brought up through make. The defaults reproduce the project's own names
exactly, so the README's boot block and test/smoke are unchanged.

scripts/rekor-tlog-health.sh asks for the pinned tree by id and reports its
absence in those words. A recreated Trillian database left Rekor answering
HTTP 500 to every request with no explanation, and the readiness report now
says which tree is missing.

scripts/backup-schedule.sh puts scripts/backup-ledger.sh on a timer, writing
outside the checkout. It was written, tested and self-tested and had never
run unattended.

Two things were found by measurement while proving this. A `down -v` also
removes Rekor's search index, and Rekor indexes an entry when it is written
and never afterwards, so the log kept the same tree and the same 25 entries
while `innsegl verify` answered "Nothing ever logged a signature over this
commit object" about a good commit. That is a false accusation rather than an
unavailable verdict, so bring-up now rebuilds the index from the log itself.
And the pseudonymisation secret turned out to belong on the protected list,
because destroying it changed what the same task string hashes to and left an
already-registered run unable to claim a commit.

Driven by OPS-031 through OPS-036, each watched failing first. OPS-031 is the
case that decides the rest. It runs a real `docker compose down -v` against a
real project and reads the real exit status, sends the same teardown through
when it touches nothing protected, holds an opt-out naming one of two volumes
to still refusing, and requires an opt-out naming both to succeed. That last
case found a real bug, since under `set -o pipefail` the opt-out released only
the last name in a list.

